WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.870
When I say “Game Company”, you think of
Nintendo, Sony, Microsoft, those who shall

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:05.870
not be named

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:11.309
Unless you have a really good memory, or really
love Chess , you don’t think of Apple.

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:12.830
And for pretty good reason.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:18.981
You can drop $13,000 on a brand new iMac Pro
and only have a decent gaming machine, and

00:00:18.981 --> 00:00:21.630
because it’s not upgradable, not for very
long.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.679
But, hey, it is space grey. (fading out)

00:00:23.679 --> 00:00:26.699
Macs can play games the same way a Smart Car
can drive cross-country.

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:29.990
Sure, it’s possible, but kinda by accident.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:35.240
Apple may have far more resources than Microsoft
or Sony, but to make a gaming platform, they’d

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.970
really have to want one, and right now, they
just don’t.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.160
At least, that’s how it looks.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.920
For many people, computers are just Facebook
or YouTube or Email machines,

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.670
For them, a phone or tablet, is, increasingly,
good enough.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:55.610
The trend is portability over performance,
which isn’t so good for the PC market.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.570
But there’s one big exception: PC Gaming.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:02.400
It has all the characteristics of a tiny,
niche market, but it isn’t.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.960
It’s actually growing.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.689
Many gamers buy parts to build their own computers,
and regularly upgrade them to keep up with

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:09.689
new games.

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:13.560
This saves a lot of money, and, for companies,
margins are pretty low.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.560
So as much as Jony Ive would love to fix this
and this, Seriously, these look ridiculous,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.020
it just doesn’t fit Apple’s strategy.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.549
Big, upgradable, machines, sold for low margins
are pretty much their nightmare.

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:29.841
Apple will pass on billion dollar industries
without blinking an eye if they don’t have

00:01:29.841 --> 00:01:31.799
something significant to add.

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:36.679
So a chart like this one looks terrible for
Apple, Android phones dominate the globe,

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:38.689
but Apple doesn’t really care because of
this:

00:01:38.689 --> 00:01:43.139
They’re happy selling fewer phones, because
it fits their strategy, they’re just so

00:01:43.139 --> 00:01:44.630
good at making profit.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.909
But that’s actually precisely why they already
are in the business of gaming, they just have

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:50.569
a very different approach.

00:01:50.569 --> 00:01:55.421
For you, gaming may look like this, or this,
but the truth is, for even more people, it

00:01:55.421 --> 00:01:56.709
looks like this

00:01:56.709 --> 00:02:03.150
Mobile gaming is a 46 billion dollar market,
more profitable than PC games, console games,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.929
even global box office sales.

00:02:04.929 --> 00:02:10.060
On any given day, Candy Crush alone makes
2 million dollars, Fornite is expected to

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.460
make half a billion dollars this year.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.540
Not bad at all.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.920
And Apple takes a 30% cut of all App Store
revenue.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.350
Obviously they aren’t only or mostly a game
company, but at this point, it’s hard to

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:23.450
deny they aren’t a game company.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.430
You might be thinking, Apple just stumbled
into this, they’re no more a game company

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:30.320
than your internet service provider is a YouTube
company.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.210
But the App Store isn’t just another app
store, it’s really the game store.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:35.440
THE game store.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.830
The App Store is 25% games, far more than
any other category.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:43.240
Last year they redesigned the entire store
to emphasize them.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.080
And game developers love the Store just as
much as the store loves them.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:52.180
Good games are expensive to make, Even what
seems like a simple game may require a team

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.290
of programmers, another for art, and music,
and writing, and marketing.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:00.000
Like movies, it’s a huge up-front investment
with a lot of risk.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.430
But the promise of digital goods is that once
they’re made, they’re made.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.390
No manufacturing, or transportation, or stocking
costs.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:11.290
One game, one click of a button, now millions
of people can download it.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:16.540
But because Google says Here’s some software,
now go make phones and manufacturers are desperate

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.950
to stand out, Android is a nightmare for game
developers.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:25.180
There are two inch Android phones, probably
two meter Android phones, and everything in

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.180
between.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.100
Do you want a phone that can tell you how
fat you are?

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.620
How ‘bout one engraved with Colonel Sander’s
face?

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.700
If you can think of it, Android has it.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.070
And they’re all sprinkled between a million
different versions of android.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:41.120
When you’re making a game, this means accommodating
tens of thousands of different phones.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.120
Not so fun.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.230
Compare that to iOS, and you can see its appeal.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.870
76% run the latest iOS version.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.570
On Android, it’s only 5%.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:54.900
This is why Apple is so pesky about updating
your phone - it adds a ton of value to their

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:55.900
company.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.840
And, sorry, Microsoft Store, it might be really
easy to develop for you, but nobody has a

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:00.840
Windows Phone.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.260
Apple also has the advantage of designing
its own processors.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.569
Which means developers can make better, crispier
games on iPhone.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:11.640
They’re kinda embarrasingly faster than
some of Apple’s own laptops.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.250
But the biggest reason companies choose the
App Store is simple: iOS users pay twice as

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.489
much for apps.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:21.940
Games like Super Mario Run and Fortnite came
to iOS first because that’s where the money

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:22.940
is.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:24.070
And Apple is very aware.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:29.510
They’ve partnered with Nintendo, Epic Games,
EA, thatgamecompany, even scarf guy.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:34.510
The iPhone, iPad, and still a product in our
lineup, iPod Touch are maybe the most popular

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:35.780
gaming devices.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:38.300
But there’s still a lot more Apple could
do.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:42.120
In 2015, Apple announced a new generation
Apple TV.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.660
Not just an expensive way to watch Netflix,
more a computer.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:51.380
It still plays Netflix, and Hulu, and eventually
Amazon, but it’s a general purpose entertainment

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:52.380
device.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:55.910
It has its own app store, with games, even
third party game controllers.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:01.770
It’s no Xbox or Wii, but it has the potential
to become the console for casual gamers.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.630
But they made one major mistake: Games had
to be compatible with the built-in remote.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.400
But the remote… well, it sucks for gaming.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.840
And because of that rule, games could only
be as good as the remote.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:13.850
(not very good)

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:18.220
Apple has now reversed that decision, but
now they’re having a chicken-or-egg problem.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:21.780
For many people, there aren’t enough good
games to buy an Apple TV,

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:25.240
but developers won’t make games until there
are more people to buy them.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.790
Sooo, which comes first?

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:30.740
And a lot of games now require a controller,
but nobody has one.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:36.139
So, here’s an idea: what if instead of selling
two sizes of the Apple TV, they sold two versions:

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:41.270
For gamers, a more expensive, higher performance,
higher storage model that comes with a controller,

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:45.920
And a slightly cheaper, lower performance,
lower storage version for everyone else.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:49.860
More people would have controllers, which
would bring more and better games, which brings

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.749
even more people, and so on.

00:05:51.749 --> 00:05:55.760
It might still be tough getting developers
to make games for the Apple TV, but there’s

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:57.610
good reason to think Apple’s working on
that,

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.740
There’s a rumor they’ll allow developers
to make one app for several platforms.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.560
So instead of having to make one version of
their app for Mac, and one for iOS, or more

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.330
likely, just doing the latter, they could
kill two birds with one stone.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:14.770
If this also worked for Apple TV, it could
bring a ton of new games to the platform.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:19.860
Apple is in a great position to create a very
profitable new gaming platform, especially

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:22.170
with how ruthless game companies can be.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:27.270
The new YouTube channel Cheddar has a video
about this very topic - how games use microtransactions

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.000
and pay to win purchases to maximize profits.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.760
It also makes an interesting counter-argument
I hadn’t really thought about - that one

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:37.510
benefit of these “free to play” games
is that they’re accessible to everyone,

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:39.180
even if you don’t have disposable income.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.740
But, as they mention, it’s reached crazy
levels with games like BattleFront II.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.000
Cheddar has very generously sponsored this
video because if you like my videos, you might

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.000
also like theirs.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.750
There’s a link in the description to watch
their video on Pay-to-Win gaming, or you can

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.930
check out some of their other videos, like
Cheddar Explains the Dot Com Bubble, a topic

00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:00.491
I’ve always heard about, but having been
born right in the middle of it, didn’t know

00:07:00.491 --> 00:07:01.491
much about.

00:07:01.491 --> 00:07:05.970
Or, Cheddar Explains the Future of Cable TV
- how younger generations are forever changing

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:06.970
the industry.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.690
All of those will be in the description!

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:11.460
And thanks again to Cheddar for supporting
the channel.

